tokyo's troubled olympic games have  suffered another setback with the  announcement that international fans  will not be able to attend  due to coronavirus concerns the summer  games were due to take place  last year but were postponed due to the  pandemic  now organizers have confirmed that only  japan based fans will be allowed to  attend  they plan to refund almost a million  tickets which had to be sold to  which have already been sold to  international fans dw reporter joel  dulroy has been following this and some  other problems surrounding the olympics  joel thanks for coming in  this decision not really unexpected but  it  did come with quite a literal shock can  you tell us more  there was a very illustrative moment  when during the meeting that led to this  decision  an earthquake struck off the coast of  japan and we can have a look now at some  images  of tokyo's governor speaking during that  meeting  as the quake strikes and you can see  here looking up at the furniture shaking  still quite composed now the quake was a  7.2 magnitude quake it  shook buildings across tokyo but it  didn't lead to any injuries or known  deaths so far  and of course the olympic committee will  be hoping for a similar  positive health outcome by making this  announcement in keeping international  guests out  in the middle of a global pandemic and  we can have a listen to  international olympic committee  president thomas bach  who explained that safety has to come  first  our first priority was is and remains  the safety of all participants  offered the olympic games and of course  uh  the uh japanese uh people uh we have uh  to take  decisions uh which uh may uh you know  need to  sacrifice from uh from  everybody  and there where we have to ask for the  understanding  there also of everybody for these  difficult decisions the ioc  president speaking about the decision  there now joel he mentioned sacrifices  how much of a financial sacrifice is  this going to be for japan  well they haven't actually announced how  much those 1 million tickets are going  to be worth  but i did some little calculations here  now previous analyses show that on  average olympic guests spend around 1  500 euros worth while traveling in a  host city  so that means japan is foregoing at  least 1.5 billion euros worth  of tourism revenue alone and these games  are already on track to be the most  expensive on record  however they had only sold around four  and a half million tickets now that is  less than the number of tickets that  were sold  for the last games at rio de janeiro and  way less than them were sold for the  london  olympics so this announcement might also  be a way of sort of saving  face and explaining away the empty seats  that we were already  we're going to see in those stadiums  also to mention while the ticket costs  are being refunded  travel costs are not so that's flights  that's hotels  so any fans who were hoping to go and  had bought tickets may be  doubly disappointed i certainly hope  everyone took out some very good travel  insurance  but this is only the latest problem of  many with this sort of  poison chalice games really if you like  tell us a bit more about that  so yes just days ago the creative  director of the games was forced to  resign after it emerged that he had  suggested a female comedian attend the  games  wearing a pig costume that became known  as the olympics  scandal and that followed previous  derogatory statements about women  made by the previous head of the games  who said that women talk too much in  meetings as a result  he had to resign so two key officials  are gone and there's another controversy  related to the olympics that you might  not know about but  the ioc has already announced in advance  that the host city for the 2032 olympic  games  is going to be brisbane australia now  that's my hometown  however well it's actually angered other  cities particularly some in the  germany's  rural rhine region now they were hoping  to make a joint bid for the games and  they are angered that the ioc has  ignored  the previous procedures of announcing  the winners of these games and  german politicians have said that they  will fight on to try and win those games  back  from australia so even though the  current host city is beset by problems  there are still  other cities around the world fighting  for the chance to host future olympics  well the battle between australia and  germany begins that is maybe not good  news for us  roy thank you very much for coming in  you
 

